{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-41706",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
